// The per-receiver fine stage: mix, filter and resample, one receiver per
// dispatch.
//
// Written from published mathematics only, per docs/clean-room.md. The
// polyphase arbitrary resampler is Crochiere and Rabiner, "Multirate Digital
// Signal Processing", chapter 3 (the polyphase decomposition) and chapter 6
// (fractional sample rate conversion by a rational L/M through a single
// interpolating prototype); the band-shifted-filter identity below is the
// modulation theorem, Oppenheim and Schafer, "Discrete-Time Signal
// Processing", chapter 2. No GPL implementation was read, fetched or
// consulted while writing this file.
//
// WHY THIS STAGE IS CHEAP, which is the reason the whole architecture works.
//
// core/shaders/pfb_branch.comp and core/shaders/pfb_fft.comp already produced
// M channels on a uniform grid, once, for every receiver in the machine. A
// receiver attaches to the nearest grid channel and undoes only the
// difference: the residual offset between what it asked for and where the grid
// put the channel, the bandwidth it actually wants, and the audio rate it has
// to land on. Everything expensive is upstream and shared. This kernel's cost
// is T complex multiply-accumulates per output sample at the demodulation
// rate, so two hundred receivers cost roughly what two cost.
//
// THE THREE OPERATIONS, AND WHY THEY COME OUT IN THIS ORDER.
//
// The obvious order is mix, then filter, then resample. Written that way the
// mixer runs at the CHANNEL rate and every filter tap needs its own phasor: T
// table lookups and T complex multiplies per output sample instead of one.
//
// The modulation theorem lets the mix move past the filter. With
// x~[m] = x[m]*exp(-j*w_o*m) the mixed channel stream, g_c the continuous
// prototype, t_j the output instant in channel samples and n_j = floor(t_j):
//
//     y[j] = sum_k g_c(t_j - n_j + k) * x~[n_j - k]
//          = exp(-j*w_o*t_j) * sum_k [ g_c(v_k) * exp(+j*w_t*v_k) ] * x[n_j-k]
//
// with v_k = (t_j - n_j) + k, PROVIDED the tap table carries the exponential.
// So the host hands down ONE complex tap table, which is the real prototype
// modulated to the filter's centre frequency f_t, and the kernel applies ONE
// output rotation per sample, at the frequency f_o being translated to DC.
//
// Keeping f_t and f_o separate is not generality for its own sake. Four of the
// eight demodulators need them different:
//
//   AM, NFM, WFM, DSB, Raw   f_t = residual,          f_o = residual
//   USB                      f_t = residual + bw/2,   f_o = residual
//   LSB                      f_t = residual - bw/2,   f_o = residual
//   CW                       f_t = residual,          f_o = residual - pitch
//
// USB and LSB are an asymmetric passband and nothing else, and CW is an
// ordinary translation that lands the carrier on the operator's pitch. The
// demodulator kernel then takes a real part for all four and does not know
// which one it is looking at. See core/dsp/vrx_reference.h, design_fine_taps.
//
// THE NCO, WHICH IS THE PART THAT GOES WRONG QUIETLY.
//
// A receiver runs for hours. `phase += increment` in float32 drifts: the
// increment is rounded once and then that rounding is added n times, so the
// phase error grows linearly with n and reads downstream as a slow frequency
// error, which looks exactly like a mistuned transmitter and is attributed to
// one. Measured against the exact phase, a float32 accumulator at 2500 Hz on a
// 48 kHz stream is 0.26 radian out after four million samples, which is 83
// seconds.
//
// So the phase is never accumulated in floating point, and it is not
// accumulated across blocks at all. It is a function of the ABSOLUTE output
// index, in exact integer arithmetic:
//
//   phi(j) = (j * D) mod 2^64,   D = round(2^64 * f_o / Fd)
//
// where f_o is the exact rational a/b hertz being translated to DC and Fd is
// the integer demodulation rate, so D is formed once by integer long division
// of 2^64*a by b*Fd with no floating point anywhere. A turn is modulo one and
// a 0.64 fixed-point turn wraps modulo 2^64, so the wrap IS the reduction
// rather than an overflow to be avoided.
//
// Two properties follow, and the second is the one that costs a day to find
// if it is missing.
//
//   The phase does not drift. D is rounded once, which makes the oscillator's
//   frequency differ from the request by at most Fd*2^-65, and relative to
//   that frequency the phase error is exactly zero at every index forever. At
//   48 kHz that offset is 1.3e-15 Hz, so after an hour the phase is out by
//   3e-11 radian. Measured against exact rational arithmetic, the phase at
//   sample 1e9 of a 2500 Hz mixer is 1.1e-10 radian from the true value.
//
//   phi(j) depends on j and on nothing else, so rendering a stream in one
//   dispatch and rendering it in blocks of seven produce identical bits. An
//   earlier version of this kernel carried the exactly-reduced phase at the
//   block's first output and accumulated a 0.32 increment from there, which
//   is accurate and is NOT this: the increment's rounding accumulated inside
//   the block, and a third of the outputs changed when the block size did,
//   because the table index straddles a boundary at exactly the phases a
//   rational frequency keeps landing on. Retroactive decode and
//   faster-than-realtime replay both re-enter the stream at an arbitrary
//   index, so an output that depends on where the blocks fell is not usable
//   for either.
//
// GLSL has no 64-bit integer here, so the host passes the phase and the
// increment as two 32-bit words each and the kernel does the arithmetic with
// umulExtended and uaddCarry, which are exact integer operations. Only the
// high word reaches the table, so the low word exists to carry.
//
// The phasor itself is a host-built table lookup, never sin or cos in the
// shader. GLSL permits several units in the last place on those and each
// vendor spends them differently, which would end bit-exactness before
// accumulation order was even in question. core/shaders/pfb_fft.comp makes the
// same trade for the same reason.
//
// THE RESAMPLER, which is also a pure function of the absolute index.
//
// The channel rate Fc and the demodulation rate Fd are both integers and their
// ratio is rarely one. Output j is taken at input instant t_j = j*Fc/Fd, so
//
//     n_j   = floor(j*Fc/Fd)          integer part, an input sample index
//     frac  = (j*Fc) mod Fd           fractional part, over Fd
//
// and the polyphase table holds P phases of the prototype, phase p being the
// prototype sampled at a fractional delay of p/P. The kernel picks the two
// phases bracketing frac/Fd and interpolates linearly between them, which
// turns the phase quantization from a timing jitter of 1/(2P) input samples
// into a second-order term and buys about 40 dB of image rejection for one
// extra multiply-add per tap.
//
// Nothing here is carried between blocks. A resampler with hidden phase state
// produces a different answer depending on how the caller chose to block the
// stream, which makes retroactive decode and faster-than-realtime replay
// impossible: both re-enter the stream at an arbitrary index. The recurrence
// below is seeded from the absolute index by the host and derived per
// invocation from i alone.
//
// The CPU twin is reference_vrx_fine in core/dsp/vrx_reference.cpp, and it is
// bit-identical rather than close. It transcribes this loop: the same
// ascending k, the same lerp-then-multiply-accumulate shape, the same integer
// recurrence including its wraps.

#version 450

// Never hardcoded; specialized at pipeline creation so one module runs on a
// device with a 64-invocation limit and on one with 1024. Id 0 is the
// project-wide convention, core/gpu/kernel.h kLocalSizeXConstantId.
layout(local_size_x_id = 0) in;

// Taps per phase, T. Specialized rather than pushed so the inner loop unrolls
// and so the tap count is visibly a property of the pipeline: changing it
// means a different filter, which means a different tap table, which means a
// rebuild anyway.
layout(constant_id = 1) const uint kTaps = 32;

// Phases in the polyphase table, P, a power of two by convention but not by
// requirement: nothing below masks by it.
layout(constant_id = 2) const uint kPhases = 256;

// log2 of the NCO table length. 16 gives a phase quantization of 2*pi/65536
// and a spur floor near -96 dBc, at 512 KiB for a table every receiver on the
// device shares. Specialized so a device short of memory can trade spurs for
// space without touching this file.
layout(constant_id = 3) const uint kNcoLog2 = 16;

// One channel's time series out of core/shaders/pfb_fft.comp. That kernel
// writes channel-major, k * out_ring_blocks + (m & out_ring_mask), so a
// receiver's reads are contiguous and the scatter was paid once upstream.
layout(std430, binding = 0) readonly buffer ChannelRing {
    vec2 values[];
} chan;

// The polyphase tap table, kPhases * kTaps + 1 complex entries.
//
// The one extra entry is not padding. Linear interpolation between phase p and
// phase p+1 reaches index p + 1 + (kTaps-1)*kPhases, which at p = kPhases-1 is
// kPhases*kTaps. That index is the prototype one whole input sample further
// out, which is outside its support, so the host writes zero there and the
// arithmetic needs no special case at the last phase.
layout(std430, binding = 1) readonly buffer FineTaps {
    vec2 h[];
} taps;

// exp(-j*2*pi*i/K) for K = 1 << kNcoLog2, built on the host from the first
// octant and reflected so the four quadrature entries are exactly (1,0),
// (0,-1), (-1,0) and (0,1). Shared by every receiver.
layout(std430, binding = 2) readonly buffer NcoTable {
    vec2 w[];
} nco;

// This receiver's complex baseband at the demodulation rate, a ring so the
// demodulator stage can reach back for the history its detector needs: one
// sample for the FM discriminator, the DC-removal window for AM, the audio
// decimation filter's support for WFM.
layout(std430, binding = 3) writeonly buffer FineRing {
    vec2 values[];
} fine;

layout(push_constant) uniform Params {
    // Origin of this receiver's channel inside the channel ring, which is
    // channel_index * out_ring_blocks. Kept as one number so this kernel does
    // not need to know the grid.
    uint chan_base;

    // Per-channel ring capacity in blocks minus one. A power of two minus one,
    // which is what makes the wrap below exact rather than approximately
    // right.
    uint chan_mask;

    // Ring offset of channel sample n_0, the integer part of this block's
    // first output instant.
    //
    // A ring offset and not a SampleIndex. SampleIndex is uint64 and GLSL has
    // no 64-bit integer here, so the host owns the absolute index and passes
    // the 32-bit offset. core/shaders/pfb_branch.comp makes the same trade.
    uint in_offset;

    // Ring offset of this block's first output sample, and the output ring's
    // mask.
    uint out_offset;
    uint out_mask;

    // Output samples this dispatch produces. One invocation each.
    uint count;

    // The resampler recurrence, all exact integers. step_whole is Fc / Fd and
    // step_rem is Fc mod Fd, so that
    //
    //     n_rel = i*step_whole + (frac0 + i*step_rem) / out_rate
    //     frac  = (frac0 + i*step_rem) mod out_rate
    //
    // reproduces floor(j*Fc/Fd) and (j*Fc) mod Fd for j = j_0 + i without ever
    // forming j*Fc, which would not fit. frac0 is (j_0*Fc) mod Fd and is below
    // out_rate by construction. The host bounds count so that
    // frac0 + count*step_rem stays inside 32 bits.
    uint step_whole;
    uint step_rem;
    uint out_rate;
    uint frac0;

    // The output NCO's phase at this block's first output, and its per-output
    // increment, as unsigned 0.64 fixed-point turns split into two 32-bit
    // words each. See the header comment: the phase is (j * delta) mod 2^64
    // computed by the host in exact integer arithmetic, so it is a function
    // of the absolute index and of nothing else.
    uint nco_phase_high;
    uint nco_phase_low;
    uint nco_delta_high;
    uint nco_delta_low;

    // 1.0f / out_rate, computed on the host so the kernel never divides.
    // Vulkan permits OpFDiv 2.5 units in the last place and requires OpFMul to
    // be correctly rounded, so a divide here could not be refereed by a CPU
    // twin at all while a multiply by a shared float can. core/dsp/convert.h
    // carries the same argument at length.
    float inv_out_rate;
} params;

void main() {
    const uint i = gl_GlobalInvocationID.x;

    // The dispatch rounds the group count up, so the final workgroup runs past
    // the end of the work; every kernel bounds-checks rather than requiring
    // callers to pad their buffers.
    if (i >= params.count) {
        return;
    }

    // Where in the input this output is taken from. Integer arithmetic only,
    // so it is exact, and it depends on i alone rather than on anything
    // carried from the previous output.
    const uint accumulated = params.frac0 + i * params.step_rem;
    const uint carry = accumulated / params.out_rate;
    const uint frac = accumulated - carry * params.out_rate;
    const uint n_rel = i * params.step_whole + carry;

    // Which two polyphase branches bracket this fractional delay, and how far
    // between them. frac/out_rate is the delay in input samples; scaling by
    // kPhases and splitting gives the branch and the remainder. The host
    // bounds out_rate * kPhases inside 32 bits.
    const uint scaled = frac * kPhases;
    const uint phase = scaled / params.out_rate;
    const uint blend_num = scaled - phase * params.out_rate;

    // `precise` is load-bearing throughout, not decoration. Every expression
    // below is the shape a shader compiler fuses into a fused multiply-add,
    // and a fused multiply-add keeps full precision on the product and rounds
    // once where separate operations round twice. Whether the fusion happens
    // is a decision each vendor makes for itself, so left alone this kernel
    // gives one answer on NVIDIA and another on AMD, both plausible and
    // neither flagged. The qualifier forbids fusion and reassociation of
    // everything contributing to the value, and the twin compiles under the
    // matching discipline with the same ascending k.
    precise float blend = float(blend_num) * params.inv_out_rate;
    precise float blend_complement = 1.0 - blend;

    const uint base = params.in_offset + n_rel;

    precise float acc_real = 0.0;
    precise float acc_imag = 0.0;

    for (uint k = 0u; k < kTaps; ++k) {
        const uint slot = phase + k * kPhases;

        // The two bracketing phases, blended into one effective tap. Doing the
        // blend on the coefficient rather than running two whole filters costs
        // one extra multiply-add per tap instead of doubling the work.
        const vec2 g0 = taps.h[slot];
        const vec2 g1 = taps.h[slot + 1u];

        precise float tap_real = g0.x * blend_complement + g1.x * blend;
        precise float tap_imag = g0.y * blend_complement + g1.y * blend;

        // (base - k) & chan_mask looks like an unsigned underflow bug and is
        // not. When k exceeds base the subtraction wraps modulo 2^32, and the
        // ring capacity is a power of two dividing 2^32, so reducing that
        // wrapped value by the mask lands on exactly the sample the unwrapped
        // arithmetic would have named. This is the ordinary case, not an edge
        // case: the first outputs after a receiver is placed all take it.
        const vec2 s = chan.values[params.chan_base + ((base - k) & params.chan_mask)];

        acc_real = acc_real + (s.x * tap_real - s.y * tap_imag);
        acc_imag = acc_imag + (s.x * tap_imag + s.y * tap_real);
    }

    // The output rotation, phi = (phase0 + i*delta) in 0.64 fixed-point turns.
    // Both words wrap modulo 2^32 and the pair wraps modulo 2^64, which is the
    // reduction modulo one turn rather than an overflow to avoid. Every
    // operation here is exact integer arithmetic, so this is the same value
    // the host computes in uint64 and the same value at a given absolute
    // index however the stream was blocked.
    uint product_high;
    uint product_low;
    umulExtended(i, params.nco_delta_low, product_high, product_low);

    uint phase_carry;
    const uint phase_low = uaddCarry(params.nco_phase_low, product_low, phase_carry);

    // phase_low is not read again. Only the high word indexes the table; the
    // low word exists so that the carry out of it is right, which is what
    // keeps the sequence exact rather than drifting by a count of samples.
    const uint phase_high =
        params.nco_phase_high + i * params.nco_delta_high + product_high + phase_carry;

    // The shift is guarded for the degenerate kNcoLog2 == 0 table, where a
    // shift by 32 would be undefined; kNcoLog2 is a specialization constant,
    // so the driver folds the select away.
    const uint nco_index = (kNcoLog2 == 0u) ? 0u : (phase_high >> (32u - kNcoLog2));
    const vec2 rotation = nco.w[nco_index];

    // Literally core/shaders/cmul.comp, the kernel this project already proved
    // bit-exact on both devices: two multiplies and a subtract, two multiplies
    // and an add.
    precise float out_real = acc_real * rotation.x - acc_imag * rotation.y;
    precise float out_imag = acc_real * rotation.y + acc_imag * rotation.x;

    fine.values[(params.out_offset + i) & params.out_mask] = vec2(out_real, out_imag);
}
